{"name":"Embroidered Silk","hex":"#b8dca7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"embroidered","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Decorated with embroidery; covered in decorative needlework.","Embellished; elaborate, especially when containing superfluous or fictitious details."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of embroider"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/embroidered"},{"term":"silk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fine fiber excreted by the silkworm or other arthropod (such as a spider).","A thread or yarn made from silk fibers."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To develop silk.","To remove the silk from (corn)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silk"}],"entries":[{"title":"Embroidery","blocks":[{"text":"Embroidery is the art of decorating fabric or other materials using a needle to stitch thread or yarn. It is one of the oldest forms of needlework, a type of textile art, with origins dating back thousands of years across various cultures. Common stitches found in early embroidery include the chain stitch, buttonhole or blanket stitch, running stitch, satin stitch, and cross stitch. Modern embroidery continues to utilize traditional techniques, though many contemporary stitches are exclusive to machine embroidery."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Kantha_%28Quilt%29_LACMA_AC1994.131.1.jpg/330px-Kantha_%28Quilt%29_LACMA_AC1994.131.1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embroidery"},{"title":"Silk","blocks":[{"text":"Silk is a natural protein fiber, some forms of which can be woven or knitted into textiles. The protein fiber of silk is composed mainly of fibroin (75-80%) and a smaller coating of sericin (20-25%). It is most commonly produced from the cocoons of certain insect larvae. The best-known silk is obtained from the cocoons of the larvae of the mulberry silkworm Bombyx mori, which are reared in captivity (sericulture)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Silk_raw_01a.jpg/330px-Silk_raw_01a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silk"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Alice_Maywood_sampler_-_DPLA_-_0e5b4f600d63fe8366c20c2ce5a90ec4_%28page_1%29.jpg/500px-Alice_Maywood_sampler_-_DPLA_-_0e5b4f600d63fe8366c20c2ce5a90ec4_%28page_1%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Alice_Maywood_sampler_-_DPLA_-_0e5b4f600d63fe8366c20c2ce5a90ec4_%28page_1%29.jpg/1280px-Alice_Maywood_sampler_-_DPLA_-_0e5b4f600d63fe8366c20c2ce5a90ec4_%28page_1%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Alice_Maywood_sampler_-_DPLA_-_0e5b4f600d63fe8366c20c2ce5a90ec4_%28page_1%29.jpg/500px-Alice_Maywood_sampler_-_DPLA_-_0e5b4f600d63fe8366c20c2ce5a90ec4_%28page_1%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Embroidery sampler by Alice Maywood, 1826","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlice_Maywood_sampler_-_DPLA_-_0e5b4f600d63fe8366c20c2ce5a90ec4_(page_1).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Bayeux_Tapestry_scene55_Eustach.jpg/500px-Bayeux_Tapestry_scene55_Eustach.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Bayeux_Tapestry_scene55_Eustach.jpg/500px-Bayeux_Tapestry_scene55_Eustach.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Laid threads, a surface technique in wool on linen. The Bayeux Tapestry, 11th century","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABayeux_Tapestry_scene55_